# agent-sandbox.rules — Linux audit (auditd) rules for watching a sandbox
# from the host.
#
# OPTIONAL. Needs root on the host. NOT one of the sandbox's barriers: it
# stops nothing, it only records. NEVER LOADED OR TESTED by the authors; read
# README.md in this directory first.
#
# auditd cannot select "the processes of one container": its rules have no
# field for a cgroup or a container. The closest it has is the user id. Under
# rootless Podman the agent's user (61000 inside the container) is, on the
# host, one id out of your subordinate range:
#
#     host id = (start of your range in /etc/subuid) + 61000 - 1
#
# So these rules see the agent processes of EVERY session of your host user,
# not of one session, and nothing of the proxy, sidecar or relay (they run
# under 31, 61001 and 61002: add lines for them the same way if you want
# them).
#
# Two placeholders to replace before loading (README.md has the commands):
#   @AGENT_HOST_UID@   the host id worked out as above
#   @STATE_DIR@        the sandbox's state directory, e.g.
#                      /home/you/.local/state/agent-sandbox (it must exist
#                      when the rules are loaded)

# Every program the agent's user starts.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve,execveat -F uid=@AGENT_HOST_UID@ -k agent-sandbox-exec

# Every connection the agent's user opens. Inside the sandbox these can only
# reach the proxy, the sidecar, the relay and sibling services; a record that
# shows any other address is worth a look. This is noisy: a build opens
# thousands, most of them to local sockets.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S connect -F uid=@AGENT_HOST_UID@ -k agent-sandbox-connect

# On x86-64 hosts, the same two for 32-bit programs, which use other system
# call numbers. Delete these two lines on arm64 if auditctl refuses them.
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve,execveat -F uid=@AGENT_HOST_UID@ -k agent-sandbox-exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S connect -F uid=@AGENT_HOST_UID@ -k agent-sandbox-connect

# Writes and permission changes under the state directory: the session
# records, the allowlists the proxy reads, the export output markers. Only
# ./sandbox should ever write here.
-a always,exit -F dir=@STATE_DIR@ -F perm=wa -k agent-sandbox-state
